    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "body": "Thanks for the question. Numba's CUDA support will permit you to do some parts of this but you'll have to write the kernels in terms of the CUDA semantics, i.e. you can't use NumPy and you have to be aware of the restrictions in place in the CUDA target.\r\n\r\nThese 3 functions:\r\n*  `resid_lstsq()`\r\n* `resid_matrixReverse()`\r\n* `resid_matrixSolve()`\r\n\r\nall look like they'd be good candidates for CuPy.\r\n\r\nPerhaps try writing those to work with CuPy and leaving your driving code in `residCalculatorTensor` on the CPU and seeing if that's good enough?\r\n\r\nIf it's not you'll probably have to write the inner loop `for trDate_j in range(trDateNum):` as a Numba kernel  that returns device arrays for `x_j_,y_i_j_` and then hand them off to the CuPy part, and then another kernel for the assignment `ResidTensor[idx,trDate_j,factor_i] = factorResid_i_j`.",
